            ]
          },
          "text": "Hi everyone! Excited to share some work from the past half year (≧∇≦)/\nhttps://t.co/QOYdAdTLFg\n\nThis is my first time splitting one idea into two papers. The companion paper, limited by re(G)sour(P)ce(U) constraints  — will come out a little later ╥﹏╥\n\nIn this paper, we study finite-horizon discrete-time Pontryagin boundary value systems and ask a simple but delicate question: can we obtain estimates that remain stable as the horizon grows?\n\nMore precisely, we prove horizon-uniform control of the linearized inverse, nonlinear local branches, and endpoint sensitivities. The key mechanism is scaled stable–unstable transversality, which leads to endpoint-corrected Green estimates. Together with weighted contraction arguments, this gives existence, uniqueness, Lipschitz dependence, and first-order expansions with constants independent of the horizon. We also give checkable symplectic, Riccati, and LQ criteria, including for genuinely coupled linear-quadratic systems.\n\nConceptually, this paper is the mathematical companion to our frozen-language-model prompting / NMI work. The NMI paper uses smooth-control identities as a fail-closed diagnostic framework for frozen LMs; this paper develops the finite-horizon Pontryagin theory that explains when such control structures have rigorous, horizon-uniform support.\n\nOur hope is that this helps turn “trajectory structure” in dynamic prompting from a useful heuristic into something more computable, verifiable, and mathematically auditable."
